  "worked_solution": "Newton\u2019s second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e89{t}^{1/2},\\,3{t}^{2}-6t\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(9{t}^{1/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-6t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{9}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,6t-6\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =4\\cdot \u27e8\\frac{9}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,6t-6\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{t}},\\,24t-24\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =\u27e8\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{4}},\\,24(4)-24\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e89,\\,72\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the resultant force acting on $P$ when $t=4\\,\\text{s}$ equals $\u27e89,\\,72\u27e9\\,\\text{N}.$",
